## std/gen — the Yield<T> algebraic effect.
##
## Operation Piezoelectric Effects, Phase 4, Milestone 4a.1.
##
## Declares `effect Yield<T>` with a single op `yield(v: T): Void`. A
## function that performs `yield` is a generator; the enclosing `with
## Yield { yield: h } do ->` installs the consumer that observes each
## yielded value. Phase 4a.3 wires the consumer-drive kind=3 state
## machine that turns a generator call into a re-entrant `$poll` frame;
## Phase 4a.4 lifts that frame into the user-facing `Stream<T>` value.
##
## Unlike `Log`, `FileIo`, and the other blessed-relaxation effects,
## `Yield<T>` has **no default handler**. Calling `yield` outside a
## `with Yield {...}` scope (or a function declaring `can Yield`) is a
## typecheck error (QZ0611/QZ0610), matching `Throws`. The design
## rationale: a yielded value with no consumer is a silent data-loss
## bug, not a runtime concern to paper over.
##
## Surface syntax for callers is `yield EXPR`, which Milestone 4a.2
## desugars to a perform-site call of this op. Until 4a.2 ships, the
## op can only be observed via `--dump-effect-decls` — that's what this
## milestone (4a.1) validates.
##
## Type-parameter note: `Yield<T>` parses but `<T>` is erased at
## runtime. Everything is i64 by the time the op reaches
## `__qz_effect_perform`; handlers decode via the usual argv slot.

effect Yield<T>
  def yield(v: T): Void
end

## Stream<T, e> — consumer-drive, row-polymorphic generator handle.
##
## Operation Piezoelectric Effects, Phase 4 Path B, Milestone 4.B.4.b
## (lifted to row-poly in Phase 4D.2).
##
## Calling a `def g(): impl Iterator<T>` function produces a
## `Stream<T, {}>` handle; an `def g(): impl Iterator<T> can Async`
## produces `Stream<T, {Async}>`. The row-var `e` propagates the
## generator body's effects to consumers — `s.next()` widens the
## caller's row by `e`. A pure caller of a pure generator binds `e`
## to `{}` (no widening); an async caller of an async generator
## binds `e` to `{Async}` (caller widened by Async).
##
##     def three(): impl Iterator<Int>
##       yield 1; yield 2; yield 3
##     end
##     var s = three()
##     s.next()   # Option::Some(1)
##     s.next()   # Option::Some(2)
##     s.next()   # Option::Some(3)
##     s.next()   # Option::None
##
## `frame` points at the underlying kind=3 `$poll` frame (synthesized
## by `mir_lower_generator_effect` via the existing async PASS 1.2a/1.3c
## machinery). `step` is a bound fn pointer to that frame's `$poll`
## function — one function per generator concrete-type, wired at
## construction by the compiler's `<name>` constructor stub. Return
## convention: POLL_READY=1 / POLL_SUSPENDED=0 / park-encoding <0.
##
## `.next()` installs a `with Yield { yield: __qz_gen_yield_capture }
## do ->` handler around `step(frame)`. Each `yield v` in the generator
## body performs kind=3 on Yield; the handler stashes v into a TLS slot
## via `__qz_gen_stash` and returns `__qz_effect_suspend()`. kind=3
## dispatch longjmps back to the with install site, so the expression
## evaluates to POLL_SUSPENDED(0). `.next()` reads the stashed value and
## returns `Option::Some(v)`. POLL_READY(1) means the body completed
## without another yield — emit `Option::None`.
##
## The `T` type parameter is erased at runtime (existential model);
## `step` and `.next()` return `Option<Int>` at the ABI level. The
## concrete type is preserved only in the typechecker for caller
## matching — `Stream<String>.next()` typechecks as returning
## `Option<String>`, even though runtime dispatches through the same
## i64-valued slot. The `step` field's `Fn(CPtr): Int` deliberately
## carries no `can` clause: step's effects are dispatched through the
## kind=3 install/perform machinery at `with Yield`, not propagated
## through the function-pointer call.
struct Stream<T, e>
  frame: CPtr
  step: Fn(CPtr): Int
end

def Stream.next<T, e>(self: Stream<T, e>): Option<T> can e
  var f = self.step
  var rv = with Yield { yield: __qz_gen_yield_capture } do -> f(self.frame) end
  if rv == 1
    # POLL_READY: body ran to completion, no value stashed. Generator
    # exhausted — subsequent .next() calls re-enter $poll, hit the
    # state=-1 done dispatch, and return 1 again.
    return Option::None
  end
  # POLL_SUSPENDED (0): handler stashed the yielded value via
  # __qz_gen_stash before longjmping back here. Take and return it.
  return Option::Some(__qz_gen_take_stashed())
end

## Yield-capture handler installed by Stream.next(). kind=3 ABI:
## `(op_name: Int, argc: Int, argv: Int): Int`. argv points to an
## `[argc x i64]` array holding op args; for `yield(v: T)` the value is
## `argv[0]` (runtime type-erased to i64). Stashes into a TLS slot via
## `__qz_gen_stash`, then returns `__qz_effect_suspend()` (0) — kind=3
## dispatch sees resumed[i]=0 and longjmps out of $poll back to the
## `with` install site, leaving the frame paused at post-yield state.
def __qz_gen_yield_capture(op_name: Int, argc: Int, argv: Int): Int
  __qz_gen_stash(__qz_effect_argv_load(argv, 0))
  return __qz_effect_suspend()
end

## Loads argv[i] (i64) from the effect-op argument array. Pure i64
## pointer arithmetic + load; emitted inline by cg_emit_effect_runtime.
extern "C" def __qz_effect_argv_load(argv: Int, i: Int): Int

## Writes `v` into the generator's outgoing-value TLS slot.
extern "C" def __qz_gen_stash(v: Int): Void

## Reads and returns the last-stashed yield value from the TLS slot.
## Called by Stream.next() after a POLL_SUSPENDED return from `step`.
extern "C" def __qz_gen_take_stashed(): Int

## Piezo P4.B.3 — POLL_SUSPENDED sentinel setter.
##
## Called from a kind=3 effect handler to park the outer `$poll` frame at
## the post-perform state WITHOUT resuming synchronously. The outer `$poll`
## invocation returns 0 (POLL_SUSPENDED) instead of 1 (POLL_READY) or a
## park-on-fd sentinel; the next poll re-enters via the state-machine
## switch at the resume block (same mechanism as a park-wake). Consumer-
## driven callers (e.g. the forthcoming `Stream<T>.next()` in 4.B.4) read
## this return as "the handler stashed a value; frame is ready for another
## drive." Scheduler-driven callers reenqueue via the task_yield branch
## (poll_result >= -1).
##
## Handler pattern:
## ```
## def my_yield(op_name: Int, argc: Int, argv: Int): Int
##   var v = load(argv, 0)
##   stash(v)
##   return __qz_effect_suspend()
## end
## ```
##
## The helper stores 0 into @__qz_effect_park_rv and returns 0, so the
## handler can `return __qz_effect_suspend()` as a tail-call idiom.
##
## Runtime emission: see `cg_emit_effect_runtime` in `codegen_runtime.qz`.
extern "C" def __qz_effect_suspend(): Int
